## Broader context
The fast-growing ‘‘Internet of Things’’ is creating ever-increasing demand for advanced batteries with high energy density, robustness, flexibility, and environmental benignity for emerging high-tech electronic technologies and electric vehicles. Zinc–air batteries (ZABs) are among the most promising alternative technologies for these applications because of their exceptional theoretical energy density, which is even comparable to that of gasoline; however, due to several critical challenges of intrinsic ZABs, such as cathode redox reactions, mechanical flexibility, and limited cycling stability, they are unable to satisfy the requirements of commercial applications. Therefore, precise fabrication of flexible, stable, and efficient electrocatalysts and superionic membrane electrolytes is of great importance. Here, we propose three approaches for integrated advanced ZABs: (1) novel 3D dual-linked hexaiminobenzene metal–organic frameworks as redox-active robust bifunctional electrocatalysts with ultrahigh intrinsic electrical conductivity and atomically tuned multishelled hollow spheres, (2) a superionic functionalized bio-cellulose nanofibrous solid-state membrane electrolyte with outstanding flexibility and water retention, and (3) a nanoporous well-patterned Zn anode. The liquid and flexible ZABs with the present constituents demonstrated world record-breaking cycling stabilities of $^{1000\mathrm{~h~}}$ (6000 cycles) and $600\mathrm{~h~}$ (3600 cycles) with fast fading and exceptionally high rate capacities (10 and $25\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ ), respectively, illustrating great perspectives for sustainable rechargeable ZABs in modern industries.
## Introduction
Rechargeable batteries with high energy density, good safety, and long cycle life represent the greatest scientific challenge of the 21st century for large-scale smart grids, electric vehicles, and wearable electronics beyond Li-ion batteries.1,2 $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}$ –air batteries (ZABs) represent a promising next-generation global portfolio storage technology because of their unique half-open systems (atmospheric oxygen fuel on air cathode), significant theoretical energy densities (1086 and 1370 W h $\log^{-1}$ for including and excluding oxygen, respectively), flexible electrodes, inherent aqueous electrolytes, environmental benignity, and abundant resources.3,4 Inferior cycle life and energy conversion efficiency are the major bottlenecks for the commercialization of rechargeable ZABs because of the sluggish kinetics of the oxygen reduction and evolution reactions (ORR/OER) governing the discharging and charging processes.5,6 Precious metals (Pt) and Ru- and Ir-based alloys have been recognized as effective catalysts for ORR and OER, respectively.7–9 However, the large-scale applications of precious metals are precluded by their global reserve scarcity, high cost, catalytic selectivity, and limited stability. Considerable efforts have devoted to investigating precious metalfree bifunctional catalysts, binary Ru–Sn oxides, transition metal (Co, Fe, Ni, and Mn) oxides, nitrides, selenides, hydro(oxy)oxides, phosphides, carbides, metal–organic frameworks, borides, and their hybrids or alloys with various forms (such as CNTs, activated carbon, carbon black, and graphite powder), as well as metal-free heteroatom-doped nanocarbon.10–20 However, because of their inherent corrosion susceptibility and inadequate electronic conductivity, their catalytic activity largely underperforms in large-scale applications compared to the precious-metal benchmarks. Flexible ZABs with solid-state electrolytes are still in the early stages of development.21–23 Complex synthesis phases, electrolyte water retention, insufficient ionic conductivity, and weak interaction with electrodes are the major shortcomings of solid electrolytes; these drawbacks limit their capacity and cycling durability.24 Therefore, the design of robust bifunctional catalysts and superionic solid-state electrolytes for ultralong-cycle-life rechargeable aqueous and flexible ZABs with fast voltage capacity fading is highly desirable.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are promising materials comprising molecular metal moieties and organic bridging ligands.25,26 MOFs are two- or three-dimensional (2D or 3D) structural motifs with high surface areas and tunable pore structures; they are promising materials for diverse applications.14,27 However, the strong inherent driving forces, weak chemical stability, inferior electrical conductivity, and specialized metals (Zn or Co) of MOFs significantly limit their lattice flexibility, electrolyte diffusion, and cycling life.8,14,28,29 Designing electrically conducting MOFs using extended $\pi$ -conjugated systems with graphene analogs is highly desirable for electrochemical reactions. The bridging of square-planar metal ions with aromatic organic moieties is a promising path to bottom-up synthesis of conductive MOFs.30,31 The abundant building blocks of organic ligands possess great diversity of the required structures because they can form $\pi{-}\mathrm{d}$ conjugated coordination polymers, including $\pi$ -conjugated ligands and metal ions. $\pi{-}\mathrm{d}$ conjugation combines the d-orbitals of metal ions and the frontier orbitals of conjugated ligands.32,33 These 2D or 3D MOFs have excellent electrical conductivity with nonzero bandgaps, illustrating the delocalization of the charges.34
The accessibility of redox sites is more prominent in 2D or 3D MOFs than in 1D conjugates because of their highly porous structures. MOFs constructed from larger ligands (such as benzenehexathiol and 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaiminotriphenylene)30,34 show restricted density of active redox sites because of their lower intrinsic density, resulting in inferior capacity and energy density. Theoretical predictions show that smaller bridging ligands exhibit more metallic behavior due to the superior intersection of the electronic wave functions of adjacent ligands and metals.31,34 Based on this premise, it can be hypothesized that metallic MOFs with hexaiminobenzene are ideal candidates. Hexaaminobenzene is a highly delocalized and conjugated organic molecule that has an excellent coordination structure with metal cations, endowing it with promising characteristics for oxygen bifunctional catalysts. The construction of these periodic skeletons with strong covalent interactions at the atomic level is a pioneering field in advanced catalysis.
The architectural design of functional materials facilitates outstanding ion transport pathways by enabling rapid access of ions to the matrix electrode. Multi-walled hollow structures enhance the surface area, electrolyte filtration, permeability, and volumetric energy density by improving the electrochemical reaction kinetics and the exploitation of active sites.35,36 The design of a universal and effective strategy for the fabrication of tunable multi-shelled hollow spheres is highly desirable. The present contribution reports two approaches: the design of robust bifunctional electrocatalysts with novel 3D dual-linked hexaiminobenzene metal–organic frameworks as redox-active conductive MOFs and superionic functionalized bio-cellulose nanofibrous electrolytes for aqueous and flexible ZABs. The periodic construction of a square-planar coordination geometry of metal species $\left(\mathbf{M}^{2+}\right)$ with hexaiminobenzene in well-defined quintet-shelled hollow nanospheres yields the benefits of numerous active sites for redox electrochemistry, high specific surface area, electrical conductivity, nitrogen species, and excellent electrode/electrolyte interfaces, which enables outstanding bifunctional performance in contrast to that of precious metals. Density functional theory (DFT) calculations show that the covalent bridging of Mn, Fe, N, and C facilitates enhancement of the surface electronic distribution and electrical conductivity via adjacent carbons, promoting fast electron transfer kinetics. Impressively, aqueous ZABs with Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF exhibited a record-breaking cycle lifetime over $1000\mathrm{~h~}$ with an ultrahigh energy density $(1027~\mathrm{W~h~}\log_{\mathrm{Zn}}{^{-1}})$ and a small discharging– charging voltage gap.
Furthermore, a superionic cross-linked bio-cellulose membrane electrolyte with high ion conductivity, excellent mechanical strength, and water retention has been designed. Flexible all-solid-state ZABs integrated with superionic biocellulose membranes and Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF displayed high energy efficiency $(65.24\%\textcircled{\div}25\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , excellent flexibility, and long-lasting cycle lifetime over $600\mathrm{~h~}$ with fast-fading capacity. This breakthrough performance of ZABs demonstrates great prospects for the practical application of rechargeable metal–air batteries in green energy systems.
## Results and discussion
The synthetic strategy of the Mn/Fe-hexaiminobenzene MOFs with quintet-shelled hollow spheres is schematically illustrated in Scheme 1. Hexaminobenzene (HAB) was used as the organic ligand. HAB was fabricated by the amination of chloroanilic acid with ethylenediamine as per our previous report.37 The black crystalline powders of the hexaiminobenzene MOFs (Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF) were fabricated by isolated reactions between hexaaminobenzene ligands and ammoniacal solutions of $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathrm{Fe(n)}$ salts under ambient air atmosphere (Scheme 1a), followed by a thermal process. The periodic bridging of the square planar coordination of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}(\mathrm{II})$ , Fe(II) and tritopic (hexadentate) HAB reveals the honeycomb layer structures of the M-HIB-MOFs (Scheme 1b and Fig. S1, ESI†). The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image of Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF shows its structural integrity achieved by constructing randomly dispersed nanospheres 100 to ${300}~\mathrm{{nm}}$ in diameter (Fig. 1a). The degree of crystallinity and unit cell parameters of the M-HIBMOFs were evaluated by synchrotron powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD). The Pawley-refined PXRD pattern with an eclipsed model shows a hexagonal crystal structure with lattice parameters of $a=b=13.25\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $c=3.16\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $R_{\mathrm{wp}}=0.0512$ , and $R_{\mathrm{exp}}=$ 0.0165 for $\mathbf{Mn}/\mathbf{Fe-HIB-MOF}$ (Fig. 1b, Fig. S2 and Table S1, $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ). The observed prominent diffraction peaks at $2.34^{\circ}$ , $5.08^{\circ}$ , $6.87^{\circ}$ , $8.13^{\circ}$ , $9.01^{\circ}$ , and $14.07^{\circ}$ correspond to the (100), (200), (120), (300), (001), and (330) Braggs orientations with the best fitting space group of $P6/m m m$ , respectively (Fig. 1b). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) showed apparent hollow spherical structures with quintet-shells (Fig. 1c). The high-resolution TEM images, fast Fourier transforms, and electron diffraction patterns (Fig. 1d–f) of Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF clearly reveal the long-range order of a honeycomb lattice structure with a pore size of $\sim8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ along with interplanar spacings of 13.26 and $3.16\mathrm{~\AA~}$ which corresponds well with the obtained PXRD results (Fig. 1b). The molecular structure of Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF was analyzed by performing scanning tunneling microscopy (STM). The STM images display well-defined nanopores of $\sim8.02\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in the hexagonal lattice array, which match well with the simulated image and DFT prediction (8 Å) (Fig. 1g–i) and illustrate the covalent construction of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}(\mathrm{II})$ , $\mathrm{Fe(n)}$ , and HIB-linked MOFs. The dark-field TEM (HAADF)- EDS mapping demonstrated the uniform distribution of C, N, Mn, and Fe along with the quintet-shelled nanospheres of Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF (Fig. 1j). Fourier transform IR spectra and thermogravimetric analysis confirmed the predicted molecular structure formation as well as the thermal stability of these MOFs (Fig. S3 and ${\bf\cal S}4,{\bf E}{\bf S}{\bf I}\dagger$ ).
The sorption isotherms of the M-HIB-MOFs show type-IV isotherms with typical H1 hysteresis (Fig. 2a), illustrating a characteristic micro/mesoporous structure. The obtained Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF was $2298\ \mathrm{~m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which outperforms those of reported MOFs and porous carbon systems.4–11,38 The Barrett–Joyner–Halenda profiles (Fig. 2b) show a main pore size of $\sim0.8\ \mathrm{nm}$ (corresponding to a DFT-calculated value of $0.8\ \mathrm{nm}\cdot$ and a pore volume of $1.63~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Numerous micro/mesopores with ultrahigh surface areas and pore volumes are key factors to enhance electrochemically active redox sites and fast ion diffusion kinetics. The coordination structures and chemical/oxidation states were determined by X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) spectroscopy. The K-edge XAFS spectra of Mn and Fe exhibited near-edge structures with a prominent valence state of $^{+2}$ compared to Fe foil. Fourier transforms (Fig. 2c and d) displayed strong signals of Debye–Waller factors for the $\bf{M n}{-}\bf{N}$ and Fe–N contributions at 1.75 and $1.83\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , respectively, confirming the successful bonding environment of one Mn(II) and/or Fe(II) with four N atoms.39 They did not show the signals for metallic pairings of Mn–Mn (2.6 Å) or Fe–Fe (2.45 Å), in contrast to Mn and Fe foils. The scattering paths of $\mathbf{Mn\cdot\cdot\cdotMn}$ and Fe  Fe at $R=3.24\pm0.1\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ and $R=3.39\pm0.1\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ (Fig. S5, $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ) exceed the actual interplanar spacings observed by PXRD, manifesting the significant shifting (AB) in the neighboring stacking layers and the lack of perfect eclipsing of the 2D layers. The PXRD and EXAFS results together confirm the successful formation of conjugated systems between radical, anionic HAB ( $\overset{\cdot}{\pi}$ orbitals) and $\mathbf{Mn(n)}/\mathrm{Fe(n)}$ (d-orbitals) for the M-HIB-MOFs.
The Fe 2p spectra of the M-HIB-MOFs displayed the major signals of Fe $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and F $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ for 710.3 and $721.4\ \mathrm{~eV}$ of $\mathrm{Fe(n)}$ ions, respectively (Fig. 3c). $^{14,47}_{,}$ The existence of abundant $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ species $(710.3~\mathrm{eV})$ indicates bridging of the active centers of the $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}{-}\mathbf{N}_{4}$ structure, which is beneficial for excellent ORR activity6,48 and consistent with the N 1s XPS spectra. The $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}2\ensuremath{\mathrm{p}}$ spectra (Fig. 3d) consist of two main peaks for 641.4 and $650.5\mathrm{eV}$ corresponding to the spin doublet of Mn $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and Mn $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ 2.21,49 In addition, the associated subpeaks of $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ were observed at 643.1, 649.7, and $652.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ . The positive polarization of $\mathbf{C-N}$ (induced by N) and $\bf{M}\mathrm{-N_{4}}$ (lower negativity of Mn, Fe) benefits the desirability of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ molecules during oxygen reactions and enables outstanding ORR and OER. The observed electrical conductivities (at 300 K) of the MOFs were 107.7, 149.2, and $359~\mathrm{S~cm}^{-1}$ for Mn-HIB-MOF, Fe-HIB-MOF, and Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF, respectively, because of the higher degree of crystallinity; these electrical conductivities are higher than those of previously reported MOFs.8,31,33,50–52 Fast electron conduction can effectively improve charge transfer at electrode/electrolyte interfaces. The electrical conductivities of all the MOFs (polycrystalline pellets) correlate positively with temperature from 200 to 450 K (Fig. S8, ESI†). PXRD (Fig. S9, ESI†) did not reflect any structural transitions in Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF with temperature. The conductivity of the bulk polycrystalline pellets corresponds more closely to the interparticle transport mechanism (i.e., thermally activated hopping over grain boundaries) rather than intraparticle transport, implying an apparent semiconducting nature that differs from that of intrinsic metallic solids. This behavior matches well with that of granular metals, where the charge transport is mediated by intergrain charge hopping. DFT band structure calculations also verified the metallic behavior of these MOFs (Fig. S44, ESI†). The retention of the high surface area with abundant active sites, advanced chemical configuration, and excellent electrical conductivity benefit diffusion of the electrolyte and oxygen and fast charge transfer, which is promising for oxygen electrochemical reactions.
The electrocatalytic ORR performance of the M-HIB-MOFs was examined using cyclic voltammograms (CVs) in alkaline environments (Fig. S10, ESI†). All the CVs displayed rectangular shapes without redox peaks for ${\bf N}_{2}$ -saturated electrolyte. Well-defined oxygen reduction signals were noted for the M-HIB-MOFs in $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ -saturated electrolyte, implying excellent ORR electrocatalytic activity. Linear sweep voltammograms (LSVs) were collected for evaluation of the ORR performance of the HIB-MOFs using a rotating disk electrode (RDE) at $1600~\mathrm{rpm}$ (Fig. 4a). Remarkably, Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF demonstrated the highest onset potential (0.98 V) and diffusion-limited current density $\left(J_{\mathrm{L}}=6.37~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\right)$ ) as well as a more positive half-wave potential $\left(E_{1/2}=0.883\mathrm{~V}\right)$ , outperforming those of $\mathbf{\rho}_{\mathrm{Pt/C}}$ (onset $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ $0.96\mathrm{V},J_{\mathrm{L}}=5.98\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ and $E_{1/2}=0.85\:\mathrm{V}_{.}$ , Mn-HIB-MOF ( $\mathrm{\mathit{J}_{L}}=$ $4.08\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ and $E_{1/2}=0.74\:\mathrm{V}_{\it\/L}$ ), Fe-HIB-MOF $\mathrm{^{\circ}J_{L}}=5.39\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and $E_{1/2}~=~0.82~\mathrm{V}]$ , other metal-based MOFs, and catalysts (Fig. S11 and Table S4, $\mathrm{ESI\dag}\$ ). $\bf{M n/F e}$ -HIB-MOF showed the smallest Tafel slope of $36\mathrm{~mV~dec}^{-1}$ , even smaller than those of $\mathbf{\rho}_{\mathrm{Pt/C}}$ $\mathrm{(42~mV~dec^{-1}}$ ), Mn-HIB-MOF, and Fe-HIB-MOF; this confirms faster ORR reaction kinetics for Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF and superior current densities with small overpotentials. Significantly, the enhanced ORR performance corresponded to fast charge transfer (i.e., lower electrochemical impedance, Fig. S12, $\mathrm{ESI\dag}_{\cdot}$ , the intrinsic activities of the $\bf{M}(\mathrm{{II})-\bf{N}_{4}}$ and $\mathbf{C-N}$ species, and a high surface area with well-constructed porous honeycomb lattice structures. The electron transfer kinetics of Mn/Fe-HIBMOF was explored by collecting RDE measurements for different rotation speeds according to the Koutecky–Levich (K–L) equation (Fig. 4b).53 The K–L plots of Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF illustrated a linear relationship with $J^{-1}$ vs. $\omega^{-1/2}$ (inset, Fig. 4b), first-order reaction kinetics for dissolved oxygen, and a transferred electron number of $\sim4$ , which is higher than those of $\mathbf{\rho}_{\mathrm{Pt/C}}$ and the individual counterparts (Fig. S13 and S14, $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ) over 0.2 to $0.7\mathrm{~V~}$ ; this clarifies the superior kinetics via $4\mathrm{e}^{-}$ ORR pathways. Rotating ring-disk electrode (RRDE) measurements were collected to extract the peroxide $\left(\mathrm{HO_{2}}^{-}\right)$ yield and transferred electron number of Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF (Fig. S15a, $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ). RRDE envisioned the transferred electron number $(\sim4)$ and a smaller yield of peroxides $(\sim4.2\%)$ , which are highly consistent with the values from the $\mathbf{K}{\-}\mathbf{L}$ equations (Fig. 4c). The dependence of the transfer number on various mass-loadings is shown in Fig. S15b and c $\bigl(\mathrm{ESI}\dag\bigr)$ . Together, these results suggest outstanding ORR activity of Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF via a dominant $4\mathrm{e}^{-}$ pathway. Electrochemical durability and excellent fuel poisoning resistance of cathodes are integral measures of their suitability for commercial green energy technologies. Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF (Fig. S15d, $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ) displayed a benchmark chronoamperometric response extended over $^{100\mathrm{~h~}}$ with retention of $97.6\%$ of the initial current, even surpassing that of $\mathrm{\bfPt/C}$ $79\%$ over $9\ \mathrm{h}$ ). Interestingly, Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF exhibited more stable retention of current density (without a distinct recession) after methanol crossover and CO poisoning compared to $\mathbf{\mathrm{Pt/C}}_{\mathrm{;}}$ implying strong resistance to corrosion and poisoning in alkaline electrolyte (Fig. S15e and f, ESI†). Moreover, the $\mathbf{Mn}/\mathrm{Fe-HIB-MOF}$ electrocatalyst demonstrated outstanding long-life stability over 10 000 CV cycles of continuous operation (Fig. 4d), whereas $\mathbf{\rho}_{\mathrm{Pt/C}}$ (Fig. S16, $\mathbf{ESI\dagger}_{\mathbf{\Phi}}$ ) suffered significant loss of its half-wave potential $(50~\mathrm{mV})$ and limited current density $(1.3\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ ; this illustrates the prospects of $\bf{M n/F e}$ -HIB-MOF for commercial applications.
The OER performance was collected to demonstrate the suitability of the M-HIB-MOFs for oxygen bifunctional catalysts. Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF was confirmed to have a lower onset- and overpotential as well as a higher specific current density compared to Mn-HIB-MOF, Fe-HIB-MOF, and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}$ , displaying superb intrinsic OER activity (Fig. 5a). Specifically, Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF showed a lower onset potential of $1.42\mathrm{V}$ compared to the $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ , Mn-HIB-MOF, and Fe-HIB-MOF catalysts. For the OER performance, Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF exhibited a significantly lower overpotential of $E_{j=10}=280~\mathrm{mV}$ for $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ versus geometric current density, outperforming $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{317~mV}$ , Mn-HIB-MOF $\left(400\ \mathrm{mV}\right)$ , Fe-HIB-MOF $\mathrm{(518~mV)}$ , and reported precious catalysts (Table S4, ESI†). Accordingly, the OER intrinsic activity follows the sequence of Mn/Fe-HIB-M $\begin{array}{r}{)\mathrm{F}>\ \mathrm{i}}\end{array}$ Mn-HIB-MOF $>$ Fe-HIB-MOF. Fig. 5b displays the smallest Tafel slope for
Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF $(45\ \mathrm{~mV}\ \mathrm{dec}^{-1})$ compared to those of $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ $(57\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1})$ and excellent recently reported catalysts (Table S4, ESI†), signifying the exceptional OER kinetics of $\mathbf{Mn}/\mathrm{Fe}{\cdot}\mathbf{HIB}{\cdot}$ MOF.54 Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS, Fig. S17, $\mathrm{ESI\dag}_{\cdot}$ ) revealed smaller charge transfer resistance for Mn/Fe-HIBMOF than for Mn-HIB-MOF and Fe-HIB-MOF. This feature is consistent with the smaller Tafel slope and overpotential, highlighting the favorable electron transfer kinetics. Positively charged carbon and $\bf{M}(\mathrm{{_{II}})-\bf{N_{4}}}$ can readily absorb $\mathrm{\o{OH}^{-}}$ during OER and transfer $\mathbf{O_{2}}^{2-}$ or ${\mathrm{O}_{2}}^{-}$ , resulting in improved OER.55 The $_{I-t}$ stability response for OER illustrates that $\bf{M n/F e}$ -HIB-MOF retained $94.8\%$ of the initial current after $^{100\mathrm{~h~}}$ , while $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ retained $82\%$ within only $^{\mathrm{~9~h~}}$ . Furthermore, Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF revealed outstanding, long-lasting stability over 10 000 continuous potential cycles; in contrast, $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ degraded severely, with an overpotential of $202~\mathrm{{\mV}}$ (Fig. S18, $\mathrm{ESI\dag}\$ . The inclusive bifunctional oxygen performance of the HIB-MOFs was determined based on the energy difference $(\Delta E)$ between the OER $\left(E_{j=10}@10~\mathrm{\mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\right)$ and ORR $\left(E_{1/2}\right)$ , where a smaller $\Delta E$ indicates outstanding catalytic reversibility.16 Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF revealed the lowest $\Delta E$ of 0.627 V (Fig. 5c), outperforming those of commercial precious metal catalysts (Pt/C, $\Delta E=1.07\mathrm{~V~}$ $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ , $\Delta E=0.91\mathrm{~V~}$ ; $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ , $\Delta E=1.23\mathrm{~V~}$ , Fig. 5d), and reported excellent metal-free or transition metal-based bifunctional catalysts (Table S4, $\mathbf{ESI\dagger}$ ).16–27 These results indicate that the unique quintet-shelled hollow conjugated system with the $\pi$ -orbitals of HIB and the $\mathrm{~d~}$ -orbitals of the metal $\mathbf{M}(\mathrm{II})$ species facilitates high intrinsic reactivity, mass transport, and high surface area with abundant electron pathways, resulting in collective enhancement of bifunctional performance.
To evaluate the bifunctional performance of Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF in air electrodes in practical ZABs, liquid primary ZABs were constructed with Zn foil as the anode and aqueous $\mathbf{KOH}\left(6\mathbf{M}\right)$ as the electrolyte. The ZABs with Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF displayed an excellent current density of $300{\mathrm{~mA~cm}}^{-2}$ for $\sim0.65\mathrm{~V~}$ discharge voltage and a high open-circuit potential of ${\sim}1.48\mathrm{~V~}$ (Fig. 6a). The Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF air cathodes showed a maximum power density of $195~\mathrm{\mW}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is greater than those of commercial $\mathbf{\rho}_{\mathrm{Pt/C}}$ $(180~\mathrm{\mw~}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ and recently reported air electrodes (Table S5, ESI†). The specific capacities of ZABs with Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF air cathodes for different discharge current densities were measured in comparison to the theoretical capacities. The specific capacities based on consumed Zn anode were 769, 742, and $702\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for current densities of 5, 25, and $50\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ with corresponding record-breaking energy densities of 1027 (the corresponding volumetric energy density is 171 W h ${\bf L}^{-1}$ based on the total volume of the cell), 930, and 788 W h $\mathrm{kg_{Zn}}^{-1}$ , respectively (Fig. 6b), which are close to the theoretical capacity $\left(1086\mathrm{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\right).$ . These results surpass those of precious metals ( $\mathbf{Pt/C},725\ \mathbf{mA}$ h ${\bf g}^{-1}$ and 920 W h $\operatorname{kg}_{\mathrm{Zn}}{^{-1}}$ , elucidating the superior bifunctional performance of $\mathbf{Mn/Fe}\mathrm{-}$ HIB-MOF.7,17,22 Notably, the galvanostatic discharge over various current densities (5 to $50\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ) exhibited stable discharge voltages without a significant drop for $^{125\mathrm{~h~}}$ , displaying a fast dynamic response, excellent stability, and superb rate capability for Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF for ORR in practical ZABs (Fig. S19, $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ . Liquid rechargeable ZABs were further configured (Fig. S20, $\mathrm{ESI\dag}]$ to quantify the OER activity of a $\bf{M n/F e}$ -HIB-MOF cathode with an electrolyte composition of $0.2\textbf{M Z n}$ acetate and $6\textbf{M}$ KOH. Fig. 6c shows the galvanodynamic discharging and charging profiles. The Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF bifunctional catalysts revealed smaller discharge–charge voltage gaps for all current densities, surpassing those of commercial $\mathbf{Pt/C}+\mathbf{RuO}_{2}$ , which facilitates excellent reversible rechargeability. The long-life robustness and efficiency of Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF for liquid ZABs were demonstrated by galvanostatic discharging–charging cycles at a current density of $10\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The ZABs with Mn/Fe-HIBMOF air cathodes displayed initial discharging and charging potentials of 1.234 and $_{1.98\mathrm{~V~}}$ , respectively, with a narrow discharge/charge voltage gap of 0.75 and a high voltaic efficiency of $62.33\%$ (Fig. 6d, e). Impressively, Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF demonstrated outstanding cycling stability for $1000\mathrm{h}$ (over ${>}6000$ cycles) without significant discharge/charge voltage fading, whereas the commercial $\mathbf{Pt}/\mathbf{C}+\mathbf{RuO}_{2}$ was constrained over $47\mathrm{~h~}$ for 282 cycles (Fig. S21, ESI†), illustrating that the long-life rechargeable durability over this excellent bifunctional oxygen electrocatalyst is the best reported to date (Fig. 6d and Table S6, $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ). Moreover, the Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF air cathodes presented competence over $^{900\mathrm{~h~}}$ (5400 cycles) at a higher current density of $20\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ with a small voltage gap of $0.78{\mathrm{~V~}}$ (Fig. S22, ESI†). The dual linkages of $\mathbf{Mn}/\mathbf{Co}$ , $\bf{M n}/\bf{N i}$ , $\mathbf{Mn/Ru}$ , $\mathbf{Co}/\mathbf{Ni}$ , ${\bf C o/F e}$ , and Ni/Fe-based HIB-MOFs displayed rechargeability over 650 h (3900 cycles), $350~\mathrm{~h~}$ (2100 cycles), $300\mathrm{~h~}$ (1800 cycles), $350\mathrm{~h~}$ (2100 cycles), $450\mathrm{~h~}$ (2700 cycles), and $333.3\mathrm{~h~}$ (2000 cycles), respectively. However, mono-linked HIB-MOFs showed cycling performance for Mn $500\mathrm{~h~}$ , 3000 cycles), Fe, (430 h, 2580 cycles), Co, (400 h, 2400 cycles), Ni, (150 h, 900 cycles), Cu, (250 h, 1500 cycles), Ir, (83.3 h, 500 cycles), and Ru ( $^{100\mathrm{~h~}}$ , 600 cycles) (Fig. S23 and Table S6, ESI†). The unique configuration of catalysts, ultrahigh surface area, efficient diffusion of oxygen species and electrolytes, intrinsic electrical conductivity, porous quintet-shelled hollow structure, and covalent coordination of the $\pi$ and d-orbitals of organic and inorganic species in a honeycomb lattice reinforced the long-lasting, excellent stability of the catalysts. Furthermore, the microscopic images (TEM and HAADF), structural analysis (XRD), and chemical analysis (XPS) clearly revealed the initial quintet-shelled structure without agglomeration (or detachments) as well as transformation to crystalline phases after long-life cycling (Fig. S24–S26, $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ). The robustness and excellent bifunctional activity of Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF meet the prerequisites for alternate redox reactions or flexible ZABs.
Flexible and rechargeable ZABs are highly desirable for next-generation portable and wearable electronics. Solid-state electrolytes with desirable mechanical robustness, high ionic conductivity, large water retention, and excellent competence with air electrodes are the critical benchmarks for flexible ZABs. The degrees of hydration and ion exchange capacities of previously reported solid electrolytes strongly constrained the capacities, energy densities, and cycling life of ZABs. The refashioning of hydroxyl groups, high surface area, strengthening, and inferior thermal expansion of nanofibrous celluloses are highly attractive for advanced ZABs. A pioneering, robust choline/chitosan functionalized bio-cellulose nanofibrous (FBN) membrane was fabricated as a solid electrolyte for rechargeable ZABs (Fig. 7a). The FBN membranes displayed bundles of nanofibrils, and a pronounced decrease in the (002) Bragg reflection phase, which enables fast ion transport through the hydrated regions by increasing the local hydrophilic domains adjacent to the hydroxide ions (Fig. S27 and S28, ESI†). The existence of comprehensive $\mathbf{O-H}$ stretching vibrations of intra/intermolecular hydrogen, bending vibrations of water in the amorphous region, and increased absorption of $\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{O}$ stretching vibrations proved the high degree of choline functionalization of the FBN membrane (Fig. S28b, ESI†). Moreover, the XPS results clearly demonstrate the functionalization of choline by showing distinctive quaternary nitrogen species at $401.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. S29, $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ). The FBN membrane displayed a superionic conductivity of $64\mathrm{mScm}^{-1}$ , outperforming those of commercial A201 and other previously reported membranes (Fig. S30 and Table S7, $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ). The highest $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ion transfer number of 0.734, large water uptake $(97\%)$ , and excellent mechanical flexibility (Table S8, Fig. S30 and S31, ESI†) manifest the superiority of the FBN membrane for flexible ZABs. Inspired by the outstanding performance of the bifunctional Mn/Fe-HIBMOF and superionic FBN membrane, we attempted to construct flexible rechargeable ZABs. Solid-state ZABs were fabricated consisting of stainless steel mesh-coated Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF as the air cathode, a unique FBN membrane electrolyte, and patterned Zn foil as an anode (Fig. S32, $\mathrm{ESI\dag}\$ ). Fig. 7b shows that the $\mathbf{Mn/Fe}$ -HIB-MOF ZABs yielded a higher power density of $194\mathrm{\mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $320\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ current density than ZABs based on $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\left(174\mathrm{\mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\right)$ ; these results are comparable with liquid ZABs and indicate outstanding performance of the solid ZABs. Also, the Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF ZABs showed superior specific capacity compared to commercial $\mathbf{\rho}_{\mathrm{Pt/C}}$ ZABs, namely $750~\mathrm{mA}$ h ${\ g_{\mathrm{Zn}}}^{-1}$ for $5\ \mathrm{\mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ with an energy density of 975 Wh $\mathrm{kg_{Zn}}^{-1}$ (a volumetric energy density of $256~\mathrm{Wh~L^{-1}})$ (Fig. 7c). Fig. 7d illustrates smaller discharging–charging voltage gaps with high current densities for the ZABs with Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF than for ZABs with $\mathrm{Pt/C}~+~\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ , signifying exceptional activity and rechargeability. The solid ZABs displayed the highest opencircuit potentials of $1.442\mathrm{~V},2.86\mathrm{~V~}$ , and $4.25{\mathrm{~V~}}$ for one, two, and three batteries connected in series, respectively (Fig. 7e and Fig. S33, ESI†). This outstanding performance stems from the lowest interfacial and charge-transfer resistances (Fig. S34, ESI†). The cycling stability of Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF/SS air electrodes for solid ZABs was demonstrated by galvanostatic discharging and charging at $25\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ with $10~\mathrm{min}$ cycles. Fig. 7f displays that the Mn/Fe-HIB-MOFs air cathode yields an initial discharging voltage of $1.22{\mathrm{~V~}}$ and a charging voltage of $1.87\mathrm{V}$ , with a small voltage gap (0.65 V) and high round-trip efficiency $(65.24\%)$ . Significantly, the Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF/SS ZABs demonstrated no noticeable voltage fading over $600\mathrm{~h~}$ for 3600 discharge/charge cycles, whereas the $\mathbf{Pt}/\mathbf{C}+\mathbf{RuO}_{2}$ ZABs showed a limited cycle life for $61\mathrm{~h~}$ with 366 cycles, indicating the outstanding durability and robustness of the Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF/SS ZABs (Fig. S35, $\mathbf{ESI\dagger}_{\mathbf{\lambda}}$ ). In particular, the ZAB with a similar air cathode $\mathrm{\left(Mn/Fe-HIB\cdot\right.}$ MOF/SS) replaced with a fresh zinc anode was also capable of additional longer cycling over $^{150\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Fig. S36, $\mathrm{ESI\dag}$ ) without significant discharge–charge polarization for a cycle time of $^{10\mathrm{h}}$ and a current density of $25\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ . This feature represents world-record-breaking long-lasting rechargeable robustness over this time-scale for solid-state ZABs (Table S9, ESI†). Microscopic analysis (Fig. S37, ESI†) confirmed that the Zn anode was dendrite-free after long-life cycling. Interestingly, the practical application of solid-state Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF ZABs was successfully demonstrated by powering red, blue, and green high-power light emitting diodes in a parallel connection with brightness overnight and live charging of a SAMSUNG Galaxy smartphone (Fig. 7g, h, Fig. S38, S39 and Video S1, S2, ESI†). These demonstrations clearly emphasize the prospective commercial applications of Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF. In addition to their outstanding cycling stability, the mechanical robustness of the fabricated solid-state ZABs was analyzed by intentional bending through different angles. Fig. 7i illustrates stable potential plateaus over discharging–charging cycles for different bending angles for a current density of $10\ensuremath{\mathrm{\mA}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{\cm}}^{-2}$ (initial discharging/charging potential $\mathbf{\Omega}=1.21/1.85\ \mathrm{V},$ ), displaying the remarkable activity and effectiveness of the flexible ZABs. Furthermore, the flexible ZABs retained their initial discharge voltages over 2000 mechanical bends for a long time (Fig. S40, ESI†). Additionally, the galvanodynamic discharging–charging polarizations demonstrated various bending prospects to analyze the bending influence. The potentials for discharging and charging remained virtually unchanged for different bent angles for all current densities compared to flat conditions (Fig. S41, $\mathrm{ESI\dag}\$ ). The EIS spectra (Fig. S42, ESI†) of the flexible tandem ZABs also displayed similar charge transfer and interfacial resistances to the projected bent conditions. This feature shows the robust mechanical integrity of the Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF electrodes and FBN membrane as well as the capability of the FBN membrane to preserve its superionic conductivity with bent projections.
Furthermore, first-principles calculations using DFT approaches were performed to provide further insights into the origin of the intrinsic bifunctional catalytic performance of Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF by investigating the OER/ORR catalytic activities and electronic structures of the M-HIB-MOFs. The optimal structures of the M-HIB-MOFs are displayed in Fig. 8a (Fig. S43, ESI†) to investigate the favorable active sites, overpotentials, and free energy pathways. The Mn-HIB-MOF, Fe-HIB-MOF, and Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF honeycomb structures exhibited well-defined planar structures without deformation along the basal planes. The intermediate structure of each elementary step in the OER/ORR mechanism of Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF is presented in Fig. 8a with the carbon active sites. Consistent with the experimental results, the theoretically evaluated volcano plots of the M-HIB-MOFs considering all possible reaction sites reveal that Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF has the best OER/ORR activity with the lowest overpotential values, even superior to conventional $\ensuremath{\mathrm{RuO}_{2}}$ and $\mathrm{\bf{Pt}}$ , as shown in Fig. 8b and c. In contrast, Mn-HIB-MOF and Fe-HIB-MOF have metal (Fe or Mn) active sites with larger overpotentials than Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF (Fig. S43, ESI†).
To understand the difference in active site preference for the OER/ORR catalytic activities of the M-HIB-MOFs and their relationships, we analyzed the electronic structures of the M-HIB-MOFs using their element decomposed band structures (Fig. S44, ESI†) because the contributions of elements to the frontier bands around the Fermi level are strongly associated with the active site preference. The element decomposed band structures show that Fe-HIB-MOF and Mn-HIB-MOF have particularly large contributions of metals to their bands around the Fermi level; however, in the case of Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF, the carbon sites dominantly contribute to the frontier bands. Consequently, Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF has carbon active sites, in contrast to the metal active sites of Mn-HIB-MOF and Fe-HIB-MOF. In addition, considering the compositional abundance of carbon in the M-HIB-MOFs, the higher contribution of carbon to the frontier bands around the Fermi level leads to increased conductivity of Mn/Fe-HIB-MOF compared to Mn-HIB-MOF and Fe-HIB-MOF, which is in good agreement with the experimental EIS results in Fig. S12 and S17 (ESI†). Hence, it is worth mentioning that the characteristic electronic features of $\bf{M n/F e-}$ HIB-MOF facilitate its OER/ORR bifunctional catalytic activities with enhanced contribution of carbons to the frontier bands and the abundant carbon active sites.
robustness of rechargeable liquid and flexible ZABs paves the way for next-generation advanced green energy storage technologies.
